{"id":1231,"date":"2026-07-02T07:19:55","date_gmt":"2026-07-02T07:19:55","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/?p=1231"},"modified":"2026-07-17T08:17:10","modified_gmt":"2026-07-17T08:17:10","slug":"%e6%b5%81%e4%bd%93%e5%a4%a9%e7%ba%bf%e7%b3%bb%e7%bb%9f%e8%be%85%e5%8a%a9%e7%9a%84%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e6%b5%81%e4%bd%93%e5%a4%a9%e7%ba%bf%e7%b3%bb%e7%bb%9f%e8%be%85%e5%8a%a9%e7%9a%84%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90\/","title":{"rendered":"Physical Layer Secret Key Generation Assisted by Fluid Antenna Systems"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Physical Layer Secret Key Generation Assisted by Fluid Antenna Systems<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Zhiyu Huang and Guyue Li and Hao Xu and Derrick Wing Kwan Ng<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Journal on Selected Areas in Communications<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>This paper investigates physical layer secret key generation (PLKG) in multi-antenna base station systems, leveraging fluid antenna systems (FAS) to dynamically customize the radio environment. Without requiring additional nodes or a large number of radio frequency (RF) chains, FAS enhances the secret key rate (SKR) at legitimate nodes by exploiting channel spatial correlation for adaptive antenna port selection. To comprehensively evaluate the performance of FAS in PLKG, this paper proposes a FAS-assisted PLKG model that integrates transmit beamforming and sparse port selection under both independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) and spatially correlated channel models. Specifically, PLKG utilizes reciprocal channel probing, and approximate SKR expressions are derived based on the mutual information between legitimate channel estimates, with the eavesdropper Eve's channel observations explicitly considered in the spatially correlated channel scenario. Non-convex optimization problems are formulated for these scenarios to maximize the SKR under transmit power constraints and sparse port activation. This paper proposes an iterative algorithm based on successive convex approximation (SCA) and the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to obtain locally optimal solutions, and employs a reweighted <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2113<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\ell_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u200b norm-based algorithm to promote sparse port activation for FAS-assisted PLKG. To approximate the optimal activated ports obtained by exhaustive search, this paper proposes a low-complexity sliding-window port selection method based on Rayleigh quotient analysis, serving as an alternative to the reweighted <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2113<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\ell_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u200b norm method. Simulation results demonstrate that the FAS-assisted PLKG scheme significantly outperforms fixed-antenna-assisted PLKG schemes in both environments; FAS achieves higher SKR with fewer RF chains through dynamic sparse port selection, effectively reducing resource overhead, and the sliding-window method closely approximates the globally optimal port selection at comparable complexity, making it suitable for practical deployment.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Key Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Two FAS-assisted PLKG models are proposed<\/strong>: one for scenarios where Eve's estimation is independent of legitimate users' estimation, and another for correlated scenarios considering spatial correlation between the eavesdropping channel and the legitimate channel; approximate SKR expressions are derived via mutual information and joint entropy analysis, explicitly incorporating the FAS port activation matrix and spatial correlation structure into the SKR analysis, and it is proven that the SKR gain increases with the largest eigenvalue of the spatial correlation matrix at the base station.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Algorithm design for solving non-convex optimization problems<\/strong>: non-convex terms are relaxed via SCA combined with the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, and a reweighted <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2113<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\ell_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u200b norm method is proposed to promote sparsity in port activation; further leveraging Rayleigh quotient theory and the oscillatory decay characteristics of Bessel-function-based spatial correlation, a low-complexity, near-optimal sliding-window port selection framework is proposed to approximate exhaustive search results, significantly reducing computational complexity.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Simulation validation and performance boundary revelation<\/strong>: the performance of FAS-assisted PLKG is revealed as an implementation boundary for achieving high SKR with minimal RF chains; results show that the scheme significantly outperforms fixed-antenna schemes in both independent and spatially correlated scenarios, and the sliding-window method consistently outperforms the reweighted <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2113<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\ell_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>norm method at the same complexity order while closely approximating the optimal selection of all ports via exhaustive search.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1232\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>System and channel model<\/strong>: a MISO system employing the TDD protocol is considered, where a multi-antenna base station Alice is equipped with a linear fluid antenna surface containing $N$ RF chains, and $M$ preset ports share these RF chains and are uniformly distributed over a linear space of length $\\lambda W$. A FAS-correlated channel model is adopted, where the elements of the spatial correlation matrix $J_A$ at Alice are characterized by the zero-order Bessel function of the first kind $J_0$, and ports can be adaptively selected via software control to exploit spatial correlation for enhancing the secret key rate.<strong>Beamforming and SKR derivation<\/strong>: Alice and Bob obtain channel state information through reciprocal channel probing combined with least-squares estimation, and Alice then constructs an equivalent channel aligned with Bob's scalar estimate via beamforming. The secret key rate $R_{SK}$ is defined as the conditional mutual information of the legitimate parties' channel estimates, $R_{SK}=I(\\hat{h}_a;\\hat{h}_b\\mid \\hat{h}^d_e,\\hat{h}^u_e)$, and approximate expressions are derived for both i.i.d. and spatially correlated eavesdropping scenarios, with the core quantity being $w^H J_A w$.<strong>Joint optimization and port selection<\/strong>: the SKR is maximized under the transmit power constraint $|w|^2_2\\le P_A$ and the sparsity constraint $|w|_0\\le N$. Since the objective increases monotonically with $\\beta_{ab}w^H J_A w$, the problem reduces to maximizing $w^H J_A w$, which is successively convexified via eigendecomposition and SCA combined with the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, with a reweighted $\\ell_1$ norm promoting sparsity. Furthermore, based on Rayleigh quotient theory and the oscillatory decay characteristics of Bessel functions, a sliding-window port selection method is proposed: a window of length $N$ slides over the $M$ ports, selecting the consecutive port combination maximizing $w^H J_A w$ as the initial vector before power optimization, reducing complexity from exponential to polynomial order $O((M+N-1)M^2)$.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-ac75e9d6 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-custom-css is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex wp-custom-css-a08f9db9\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-3add2428 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/b7fe770b-9274-4275-b440-d36801098f17.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/b7fe770b-9274-4275-b440-d36801098f17.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/b7fe770b-9274-4275-b440-d36801098f17.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/b7fe770b-9274-4275-b440-d36801098f17.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1238\" width=\"773\" height=\"551\" title=\"b7fe770b-9274-4275-b440-d36801098f17\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-132ce920 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/30c326e9-5a2c-4bd7-a5fd-f9dd7153ff12.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/30c326e9-5a2c-4bd7-a5fd-f9dd7153ff12.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/30c326e9-5a2c-4bd7-a5fd-f9dd7153ff12.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/30c326e9-5a2c-4bd7-a5fd-f9dd7153ff12.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1239\" width=\"778\" height=\"527\" title=\"30c326e9-5a2c-4bd7-a5fd-f9dd7153ff12\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-9104dc44 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/0e026b36-7e9a-40ad-bb50-08992f8381f3.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/0e026b36-7e9a-40ad-bb50-08992f8381f3.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/0e026b36-7e9a-40ad-bb50-08992f8381f3.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/0e026b36-7e9a-40ad-bb50-08992f8381f3.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1241\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"0e026b36-7e9a-40ad-bb50-08992f8381f3\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-9b091da0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7b6ecadf-1ab7-45c5-8a60-3ac2b17c93f3-1024x660.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7b6ecadf-1ab7-45c5-8a60-3ac2b17c93f3.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7b6ecadf-1ab7-45c5-8a60-3ac2b17c93f3.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/7b6ecadf-1ab7-45c5-8a60-3ac2b17c93f3-1024x660.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1234\" width=\"1061\" height=\"684\" title=\"7b6ecadf-1ab7-45c5-8a60-3ac2b17c93f3\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>FAS-assisted PLKG significantly outperforms fixed-antenna schemes in both channel environments and achieves higher secret key rates with fewer RF chains<\/strong>: as shown in Fig. 3 and Fig. 4, the SKR increases monotonically with the power budget $P_A$ and gradually saturates; the FAS scheme achieves or even exceeds the performance of fixed-antenna schemes with more activated antennas (\u201dFA Opt, $N=7$\u201d) using fewer activated antennas (\u201dFAS Opt, $N=5$\u201d). Regarding the impact of the number of RF chains (Fig. 8, Fig. 9), the SKR improves monotonically as $N$ increases, with the spatially correlated scenario showing a 38.2% SKR increase while the i.i.d. scenario increases only 4.2%, indicating significantly higher sensitivity to the number of RF chains in the former. Additionally, the sliding-window method (\u201dFAS Sliding Window\u201d) consistently outperforms the reweighted $\\ell_1$ norm method (\u201dFAS Opt\u201d) in terms of convergence, key disagreement rate (KDR, Fig. 7), and the impact of normalized size $W$ (Fig. 12, Fig. 13), while closely approximating exhaustive search (\u201dFAS Traverse\u201d); in robustness tests (Fig. 16, Fig. 17), the FAS scheme degrades more slowly as the spatial correlation matrix estimation error $\\sigma^2_\\Delta$ increases. In terms of convergence, approximately 15 iterations are required for $M=32$ and about 10 for $M=16$.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Cite This Paper<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{huang2026fluid,\n  author    = {Zhiyu Huang and Guyue Li and Hao Xu and Derrick Wing Kwan Ng},\n  title     = {Fluid Antenna System-Assisted Physical Layer Secret Key Generation},\n  journal   = {IEEE Journal on Selected Areas in Communications},\n  volume    = {44},\n  pages     = {4429--4443},\n  year      = {2026},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/JSAC.2026.3679311}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>This paper investigates physical layer secret key generation (PLKG) in multi-antenna base station systems, leveraging the Fluid Antenna System (FAS) to dynamically customize the radio environment. Without requiring additional nodes or a large number of radio frequency (RF) chains, FAS enhances the secret key rate (SKR) at legitimate nodes by exploiting channel spatial correlation for adaptive antenna port selection. To comprehensively evaluate the performance of FAS in PLKG, this paper proposes a FAS-assisted PLKG model that integrates transmit beamforming and sparse port selection under both independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) and spatially correlated channel models. Specifically, PLKG utilizes reciprocal channel probing, deriving an approximate SKR expression based on the mutual information between legitimate channel estimates, and explicitly considers the channel observations of the eavesdropper, Eve, in the spatially correlated channel scenario. Non-convex optimization problems are formulated for these scenarios to maximize SKR under transmit power constraints and sparse port activation. This paper proposes an iterative algorithm based on successive convex approximation (SCA) and the Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality to obtain locally optimal solutions, and employs a reweighted \u21131\\ell_1\u21131\u200b-norm-based algorithm to promote sparse port activation for FAS-assisted PLKG. To approximate the optimal activated ports obtained by exhaustive search, this paper proposes a low-complexity sliding window port selection method based on Rayleigh quotient analysis, serving as an alternative to the reweighted \u21131\\ell_1\u21131\u200b-norm method. Simulation results demonstrate that the FAS-assisted PLKG scheme significantly outperforms the fixed-antenna-assisted PLKG scheme in both environments; FAS achieves higher SKR with fewer RF chains through dynamic sparse port selection, effectively reducing resource overhead, and the sliding window method closely approximates the globally optimal port selection at comparable complexity, making it suitable for practical deployment.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1232,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1231","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png",918,505,false],"thumbnail":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3-150x150.png",150,150,true],"medium":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3-300x165.png",300,165,true],"medium_large":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3-768x422.png",768,422,true],"large":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png",918,505,false],"1536x1536":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png",918,505,false],"2048x2048":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png",918,505,false],"trp-custom-language-flag":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/01655daf-a505-4698-8dbe-34a41cc10ca3.png",18,10,false]},"uagb_author_info":{"display_name":"jiushihema","author_link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/author\/jiushihema\/"},"uagb_comment_info":0,"uagb_excerpt":"\u672c\u6587\u7814\u7a76\u591a\u5929\u7ebf\u57fa\u7ad9\